<worksheet xml:id="subsection-abs-extrema">
    <title> Exercises for Extreme Values and Saddle Points</title>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-extrema-triangle">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of
            <md>f(x,y)=x^2-xy+y^2-3y</md>
            on the region bounded by the <m>x</m>- and
            <m>y</m>-axes and the line <m>x+y=4</m>.
          </p>

          <p component="print">
            An interactive step-by-step hint for this problem accompanies the
            online version of these notes.
          </p>
          <sidebyside component="web" width="80%">
          <interactive
            xml:id="hints-extrema-triangle"
            width="100%"
            aspect="5:4"
            iframe="iframe/hint-extrema-triangle.html"/>
          </sidebyside>
        </statement>

         <solution>
      <p>
        The gradient is <m>\nabla f = \langle 2x - y,\; -x + 2y - 3\rangle</m>, which
        vanishes only at <m>(1,2)</m>, a point in the interior of the region. There
        <m>f(1,2) = -3</m>. Since this is the only critical point on all of
        <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> and it is a minimum (by the second derivative test), it is a
        global minimum. We now examine the three boundary segments to look for possible
        maxima.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the <m>x</m>-axis with <m>0 \le x \le 4</m>, we have <m>f(x,0) = x^2</m>, which
        is maximized at <m>f(4,0) = 16</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the <m>y</m>-axis with <m>0 \le y \le 4</m>, we have <m>f(0,y) = y^2 - 3y</m>,
        which is maximized at <m>f(0,4) = 4</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, on the line <m>x = 4 - y</m> with <m>0 \le y \le 4</m>, we have
        <m>f(4-y,\,y) = 3y^2 - 15y + 16</m>, which is maximized at <m>f(4,0) = 16</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Altogether, there is an absolute minimum <m>f(1,2) = -3</m> and an absolute maximum
        <m>f(4,0) = 16</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
      </exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-ex-1">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="no">
<p>
If <m>f(x,y)</m> has two local maxima, then it must have a local
minimum too.
</p>
<p>
Justify your answer fully: give a proof if the statement is true, or a
counterexample if it is false.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
This is true for single-variable functions but no
longer holds in two dimensions. For example, the function
<m>f(x,y) = -x^4 + x^2 - \frac12 y^2</m>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-rm2-two-maxima"/>, has two local maxima at
<m>\left(\pm\frac{1}{\sqrt2}, 0\right)</m> with a saddle point at the
origin lying between them, and no local minimum.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rm2-two-maxima">
<caption>
The graph of <m>f(x,y) = -x^4 + x^2 - \frac12 y^2</m>: two local
maxima with a saddle point between them, and no local minimum.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
A three dimensional surface with two rounded peaks of equal height,
one on the left and one on the right, separated by a saddle shaped
dip in the middle. Away from the peaks the surface falls off in every
direction, so there is no lowest point.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=10.5cm, height=7.5cm,
  view={-40}{28},
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  ticks=none,
  clip=false,
]
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=gray!45, draw=gray!60,
          fill opacity=0.55, draw opacity=0.45,
          domain=-1.15:1.15, y domain=-1.05:1.05,
          samples=17, samples y=13]
  {-x^4 + x^2 - 0.5*y^2};
% the two maxima
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.7, color=purple!70!black]
  coordinates {(-0.7071,0,0.25) (0.7071,0,0.25)};
% the saddle point
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.7, color=blue!60!black]
  coordinates {(0,0,0)};
\node[above, purple!70!black] at (axis cs:-0.7071,0,0.30) {max};
\node[above, purple!70!black] at (axis cs:0.7071,0,0.30) {max};
\node[above right, blue!60!black] at (axis cs:0,0,0.02) {saddle};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-ex-2">
<statement>
<p>
Find and classify all the critical points of
<m>f(x,y) = xy\,e^{x^2+y^2}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The gradient is
<md>
\nabla f(x,y) = \left\langle
\left(1 + 2x^2\right) y\, e^{x^2+y^2},\;
x \left(1 + 2y^2\right) e^{x^2+y^2}
\right\rangle.
</md>
Since the exponential factors, as well as <m>1 + 2x^2</m> and
<m>1 + 2y^2</m>, are always positive, we might as well ignore them
when finding the zeros. So we are left with
<m>\langle x, y\rangle = \langle 0, 0\rangle</m>, i.e. the only
critical point is <m>(0,0)</m>. The second derivatives of <m>f</m> are
<md>
<mrow>f_{xx} \amp = \left(6x + 4x^3\right) y\, e^{x^2+y^2}</mrow>
<mrow>f_{xy} \amp = \left(1 + 2x^2\right)\left(1 + 2y^2\right)
e^{x^2+y^2}</mrow>
<mrow>f_{yy} \amp = x\left(6y + 4y^3\right) e^{x^2+y^2}</mrow>
</md>
Hence <m>D(0,0) = f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 = -1</m>, so <m>(0,0)</m> is
a saddle point.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise xml:id="rs19-8">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>f(x,y) = x^2 - y^2 - 2x + 4y + 6</m>. Find all the critical
points and classify them as local maxima, local minima, and saddle
points.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The first and second partial derivatives are
<md>
<mrow>f_x \amp = 2x - 2, \amp f_{xx} \amp = 2,
\amp f_{xy} \amp = f_{yx} = 0,</mrow>
<mrow>f_y \amp = -2y + 4, \amp f_{yy} \amp = -2.</mrow>
</md>
Setting <m>f_x = 0</m> gives <m>x = 1</m> and <m>f_y = 0</m> gives
<m>y = 2</m>, so the only critical point is <m>(1,2)</m>. The Hessian
is
<md>
H(x,y) =
\begin{vmatrix}
f_{xx} \amp f_{xy} \\
f_{xy} \amp f_{yy}
\end{vmatrix}
=
\begin{vmatrix}
2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -2
\end{vmatrix}
= -4 \lt 0,
</md>
so <m>(1,2)</m> is a saddle point since the Hessian is negative.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw21-7">
<statement>
<p>
Find the maximum and minimum values of the function
<m>f(x,y) = 2x^2 + 3y^2 - 4x - 5</m> on the domain
<m>x^2 + y^2 \le 121</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<em>Critical points in the interior:</em>
<md>
f_x = 4x - 4 = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; x = 1,
\qquad
f_y = 6y = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; y = 0,
</md>
so <m>(1,0)</m> is the only critical point, with
<m>f(1,0) = -7</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>The boundary:</em> on the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 121</m> we have
<m>y^2 = 121 - x^2</m> with <m>-11 \le x \le 11</m>, so
<md>
f = 2x^2 + 3\left(121 - x^2\right) - 4x - 5
= 358 - 4x - x^2,
</md>
and
<md>
\frac{d}{dx}\left(358 - 4x - x^2\right) = -4 - 2x = 0
\;\Rightarrow\; x = -2,
</md>
giving <m>y^2 = 117</m>, i.e. <m>y = \pm\sqrt{117}</m>, with
<md>
f\left(-2, \pm\sqrt{117}\right) = 358 + 8 - 4 = 362.
</md>
At the endpoints <m>x = \pm 11</m> (where <m>y = 0</m>),
<m>f(11,0) = 193</m> and <m>f(-11,0) = 281</m>.
</p>

<p>
Comparing all the values, the maximum value of <m>f</m> is
<m>362</m>, attained at <m>\left(-2, \sqrt{117}\right)</m> and
<m>\left(-2, -\sqrt{117}\right)</m>, and the minimum value is
<m>-7</m>, attained at <m>(1,0)</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-rw21-disk"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rw21-disk">
<caption>
The disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 121</m>, the interior minimum at
<m>(1,0)</m>, and the boundary maxima at
<m>\left(-2, \pm\sqrt{117}\right)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="56%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rw21-disk">
<diagram dimensions="(300,290)" margins="12">
<definition>circ(t) = (11*cos(t), 11*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-15.5,-14.5,15.5,14.5)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="dk-circle" function="circ" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<point at="dk-min" p="(1,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<point at="dk-max1" p="(-2,10.8167)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="dk-max2" p="(-2,-10.8167)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<label at="dk-min-lab" anchor="(1.6,-2.6)" alignment="se" color="#009E73">
<m>(1,0),\ f=-7</m>
</label>
<label at="dk-max1-lab" anchor="(-14.8,12.1)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>\left(-2,\sqrt{117}\right),\ f=362</m>
</label>
<label at="dk-max2-lab" anchor="(-14.8,-13.2)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>\left(-2,-\sqrt{117}\right),\ f=362</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blue circle of radius eleven centered at the origin. A green point just right of the center at one comma zero marks the minimum, f equals negative seven. Two vermillion points on the circle at negative two comma plus and minus the square root of one hundred seventeen mark the maxima, f equals three hundred sixty two.">
  <annotation ref="dk-circle" text="The boundary circle x squared plus y squared equals one hundred twenty one."/>
  <annotation ref="dk-min" text="The interior critical point one comma zero, where the minimum negative seven occurs."/>
  <annotation ref="dk-max1" text="A boundary maximum at negative two comma square root of one hundred seventeen."/>
  <annotation ref="dk-max2" text="A boundary maximum at negative two comma negative square root of one hundred seventeen."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw21-9">
<statement>
<p>
Find the absolute maximum and absolute minimum of
<m>f(x,y) = 2 + xy - 2x - \frac14 y^2</m> in the closed triangular
region with vertices given by <m>(0,0)</m>, <m>(1,0)</m>, and
<m>(0,2)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<em>Critical points:</em>
<md>
f_x = y - 2 = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; y = 2,
\qquad
f_y = x - \frac{y}{2} = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; x = 1,
</md>
so the only critical point is <m>(1,2)</m>. Note that this point is
NOT in the closed region <m>R</m>, so we only need to check the
boundary.
</p>

<p>
<em>The boundary:</em> we check the three sides of the triangle.
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>L_1</m>: <m>x = 0</m>, <m>0 \le y \le 2</m>. Here
<m>f(0,y) = 2 - \frac{y^2}{4}</m>, with maximum <m>2</m> at
<m>(0,0)</m> and minimum <m>1</m> at <m>(0,2)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>L_2</m>: <m>y = 0</m>, <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>. Here
<m>f(x,0) = 2 - 2x</m>, with maximum <m>2</m> at <m>(0,0)</m> and
minimum <m>0</m> at <m>(1,0)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>L_3</m>: <m>y = -2x + 2</m>, <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>. Here
<md>
g(x) = f(x, -2x+2) = 2 - 2x^2 - (x-1)^2 = 1 - 3x^2 + 2x,
</md>
and <m>g'(x) = -6x + 2 = 0</m> gives <m>x = \frac13</m>,
<m>y = \frac43</m>, with
<m>f\left(\frac13, \frac43\right)
= 2 + \frac49 - \frac23 - \frac49 = \frac43</m>. The endpoints of
<m>L_3</m> give the previously found values <m>1</m> and <m>0</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>
Comparing all the values, the absolute maximum of <m>f</m> is
<m>2</m>, attained at <m>(0,0)</m>, and the absolute minimum is
<m>0</m>, attained at <m>(1,0)</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-rw21-triangle"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rw21-triangle">
<caption>
The triangular region <m>R</m> with vertices <m>(0,0)</m>,
<m>(1,0)</m>, and <m>(0,2)</m>. The critical point <m>(1,2)</m> lies
outside <m>R</m>; the absolute maximum <m>2</m> occurs at
<m>(0,0)</m> and the absolute minimum <m>0</m> at <m>(1,0)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="56%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rw21-triangle">
<diagram dimensions="(300,280)" margins="12">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.6,-0.7,2.6,2.7)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<polygon at="tr-region" points="((0,0),(1,0),(0,2))"
         fill="#56B4E9" fill-opacity="0.35"
         stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<point at="tr-max" p="(0,0)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="tr-min" p="(1,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<point at="tr-crit" p="(1,2)" fill="#8B1A4A"/>
<label at="tr-max-lab" anchor="(-0.1,-0.15)" alignment="sw" color="#D55E00">
<m>(0,0),\ f=2</m>
</label>
<label at="tr-min-lab" anchor="(1.1,-0.15)" alignment="se" color="#009E73">
<m>(1,0),\ f=0</m>
</label>
<label at="tr-crit-lab" anchor="(1.08,2.1)" alignment="ne" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>(1,2)\notin R</m>
</label>
<label at="tr-R-lab" anchor="(0.28,0.55)" alignment="ne" color="#0072B2">
<m>R</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A light blue right triangle with vertices at the origin, one comma zero, and zero comma two. The vertex at the origin is marked in vermillion as the absolute maximum, f equals two; the vertex at one comma zero is marked in green as the absolute minimum, f equals zero. A dark red point at one comma two, outside the triangle, marks the critical point that does not belong to the region.">
  <annotation ref="tr-region" text="The closed triangular region R."/>
  <annotation ref="tr-max" text="The absolute maximum at the origin, where f equals two."/>
  <annotation ref="tr-min" text="The absolute minimum at one comma zero, where f equals zero."/>
  <annotation ref="tr-crit" text="The critical point one comma two, which lies outside the region."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw23-4">
<statement>
<p>
The business manager for Acme Corporation plots a grid on a map of the
region Acme serves and determines that the company's three most
important customers are located at points <m>A(1,5)</m>,
<m>B(0,0)</m>, and <m>C(8,0)</m>, where units are in miles. At what
point <m>W(x,y)</m> should a warehouse be located in order to minimize
<em>the sum of the squares of the distances</em> (NOT the square of
the sum) from <m>W</m> to <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m>?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The sum of the squares of the distances is
<md>
<mrow>f(x,y) \amp = (x-1)^2 + (y-5)^2 + x^2 + y^2 + (x-8)^2 + y^2</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 3x^2 - 18x + 3y^2 - 10y + 90.</mrow>
</md>
Setting the partial derivatives equal to zero,
<md>
<mrow>f_x \amp = 6x - 18 = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; x = 3,</mrow>
<mrow>f_y \amp = 6y - 10 = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; y = \frac53,</mrow>
</md>
so <m>\left(3, \frac53\right)</m> is the only critical point. The
second derivatives are <m>f_{xx} = 6 \gt 0</m>, <m>f_{yy} = 6</m>,
<m>f_{xy} = 0</m>, so
<m>D = f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 = 36 \gt 0</m>. Since <m>D \gt 0</m>
and <m>f_{xx} \gt 0</m>, the point <m>\left(3, \frac53\right)</m> is a
local minimum.
</p>

<p>
Also, if we complete the squares we can see that this point is where
the absolute minimum happens:
<md>
f(x,y) = 3\left(y - \frac53\right)^2 + 3(x-3)^2
+ \underbrace{90 - \frac{25}{3} - 27}_{\gt 0},
</md>
where the first two terms are non-negative and vanish exactly at
<m>\left(3, \frac53\right)</m>. See <xref ref="fig-rw23-warehouse"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rw23-warehouse">
<caption>
The three customers <m>A(1,5)</m>, <m>B(0,0)</m>, <m>C(8,0)</m>, and
the warehouse location <m>W\left(3, \frac53\right)</m> that minimizes
the sum of the squares of the distances.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rw23-warehouse">
<diagram dimensions="(360,260)" margins="12">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.2,-1.2,9.2,6.2)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<line at="wh-wa" endpoints="((3,1.6667),(1,5))"
      stroke="#009E73" dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="wh-wb" endpoints="((3,1.6667),(0,0))"
      stroke="#009E73" dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="wh-wc" endpoints="((3,1.6667),(8,0))"
      stroke="#009E73" dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<point at="wh-A" p="(1,5)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="wh-B" p="(0,0)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="wh-C" p="(8,0)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="wh-W" p="(3,1.6667)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<label at="wh-A-lab" anchor="(1.15,5.15)" alignment="ne" color="#0072B2">
<m>A(1,5)</m>
</label>
<label at="wh-B-lab" anchor="(0.15,-0.35)" alignment="se" color="#0072B2">
<m>B(0,0)</m>
</label>
<label at="wh-C-lab" anchor="(7.9,-0.35)" alignment="sw" color="#0072B2">
<m>C(8,0)</m>
</label>
<label at="wh-W-lab" anchor="(3.25,1.85)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
<m>W\!\left(3,\frac53\right)</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Three blue points mark the customers: A at one comma five, B at the origin, and C at eight comma zero. A vermillion point W at three comma five thirds sits between them, joined to each customer by a dashed green segment.">
  <annotation ref="wh-W" text="The warehouse location W at three comma five thirds, the point minimizing the sum of the squares of the distances."/>
  <annotation ref="wh-A" text="Customer A at one comma five."/>
  <annotation ref="wh-B" text="Customer B at the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="wh-C" text="Customer C at eight comma zero."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise xml:id="rw17-7">
<statement>
<p>
The porosity of the soil beneath an elliptical section of a field,
<m>x^2 + 4y^2 \le 25</m>, is given by
<md>
f(x,y) = 2x^2 - 9x + 2y^2 + 20.
</md>
Find the <m>(x,y)</m> location(s) in this section of the field where
water will drain the fastest and slowest, i.e. the locations where
porosity is an absolute maximum or absolute minimum.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The region is closed and bounded and <m>f</m> is a polynomial, hence
continuous, so by the Extreme Value Theorem absolute extrema exist. We
check the interior and the boundary.
</p>

<p>
<em>Interior critical points:</em>
<md>
f_x = 4x - 9 = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; x = \frac94,
\qquad
f_y = 4y = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; y = 0.
</md>
The point <m>\left(\frac94, 0\right)</m> satisfies
<m>\left(\frac94\right)^2 = \frac{81}{16} \approx 5.06 \lt 25</m>, so
it lies inside the region, and
<md>
<mrow>f\left(\frac94, 0\right)
\amp = 2\left(\frac{81}{16}\right) - 9\left(\frac94\right) + 20</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{81}{8} - \frac{81}{4} + 20
= -\frac{81}{8} + 20
= \frac{79}{8} = 9.875.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
<em>The boundary:</em> on <m>x^2 + 4y^2 = 25</m> we have
<m>y^2 = \dfrac{25 - x^2}{4}</m> with <m>-5 \le x \le 5</m>, so
<md>
f = 2x^2 - 9x + 2\cdot\frac{25-x^2}{4} + 20
= \frac32 x^2 - 9x + \frac{65}{2},
</md>
a function of the single variable <m>x</m>. Setting its derivative to
zero,
<md>
3x - 9 = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; x = 3
\;\Rightarrow\; y^2 = \frac{25-9}{4} = 4
\;\Rightarrow\; y = \pm 2,
</md>
with <m>f(3, \pm 2) = \frac{27}{2} - 27 + \frac{65}{2} = 19</m>. At
the endpoints of the interval,
<md>
f(5, 0) = \frac{75}{2} - 45 + \frac{65}{2} = 25,
\qquad
f(-5, 0) = \frac{75}{2} + 45 + \frac{65}{2} = 115.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Comparing all the values <m>\left\{\frac{79}{8}, 19, 25, 115\right\}</m>,
the porosity is an absolute <em>maximum</em> of <m>115</m> at
<m>(-5, 0)</m> — where water drains the fastest — and an absolute
<em>minimum</em> of <m>\frac{79}{8}</m> at
<m>\left(\frac94, 0\right)</m> — where water drains the slowest. See
<xref ref="fig-rw17-porosity"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rw17-porosity">
<caption>
The elliptical section <m>x^2 + 4y^2 \le 25</m>. The porosity is
largest at the boundary point <m>(-5,0)</m> and smallest at the
interior critical point <m>\left(\frac94, 0\right)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rw17-porosity">
<diagram dimensions="(360,220)" margins="12">
<definition>ell(t) = (5*cos(t), 2.5*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-6.6,-3.6,6.6,3.6)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="po-ell" function="ell" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<point at="po-max" p="(-5,0)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="po-min" p="(2.25,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<point at="po-b1" p="(3,2)" fill="#7F7F7F"/>
<point at="po-b2" p="(3,-2)" fill="#7F7F7F"/>
<label at="po-max-lab" anchor="(-5,0.25)" alignment="n" color="#D55E00">
<m>(-5,0),\ f=115</m>
</label>
<label at="po-min-lab" anchor="(2.25,-0.3)" alignment="s" color="#009E73">
<m>\left(\frac94,0\right),\ f=\frac{79}{8}</m>
</label>
<label at="po-b1-lab" anchor="(2.85,2.2)" alignment="nw" color="#7F7F7F">
<m>f=19</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A wide blue ellipse with semi axes five in x and two point five in y. A vermillion point at negative five comma zero on the left end marks the maximum porosity of one hundred fifteen. A green point inside at nine fourths comma zero marks the minimum porosity of seventy nine eighths. Two gray points on the boundary at three comma plus and minus two are the boundary critical points where f equals nineteen.">
  <annotation ref="po-ell" text="The boundary ellipse x squared plus four y squared equals twenty five."/>
  <annotation ref="po-max" text="The absolute maximum of the porosity, at negative five comma zero."/>
  <annotation ref="po-min" text="The absolute minimum of the porosity, at the interior critical point nine fourths comma zero."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-35">
<statement>
<p>
Find all local maxima, minima and saddle points for
<m>f(x,y) = \frac13 x^3 - x + xy^2</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The first partials are
<md>
f_x = x^2 - 1 + y^2,
\qquad
f_y = 2xy.
</md>
Setting <m>f_y = 0</m> forces <m>x = 0</m> or <m>y = 0</m>.
<ul>
<li>
<p>
If <m>x = 0</m>: <m>f_x = -1 + y^2 = 0</m> gives <m>y = \pm 1</m>, so
the critical points are <m>(0,1)</m> and <m>(0,-1)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
If <m>y = 0</m>: <m>f_x = x^2 - 1 = 0</m> gives <m>x = \pm 1</m>, so
the critical points are <m>(1,0)</m> and <m>(-1,0)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>
The second partials are <m>f_{xx} = 2x</m>, <m>f_{yy} = 2x</m>, and
<m>f_{xy} = 2y</m>, so the discriminant is
<md>
D = f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^{2} = 4x^2 - 4y^2.
</md>
Applying the second derivative test:
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>(0, \pm 1)</m>: <m>D = -4 \lt 0</m>, so both are
<term>saddle points</term>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>(1,0)</m>: <m>D = 4 \gt 0</m> and <m>f_{xx} = 2 \gt 0</m>, so this
is a <term>local minimum</term>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>(-1,0)</m>: <m>D = 4 \gt 0</m> and <m>f_{xx} = -2 \lt 0</m>, so
this is a <term>local maximum</term>.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>

